{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 41, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network (2010) is a masterclass in modern filmmaking, blending razor-sharp dialogue, meticulous storytelling, and a hauntingly atmospheric soundtrack into a near-perfect cinematic experience. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film dissects the birth of facebook and the tumultuous relationships that fueled its creation with a precision that feels almost surgical.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, capturing the character’s genius, arrogance, and emotional detachment with unsettling accuracy. his portrayal is a tightrope walk between charm and cruelty, making zuckerberg both fascinating and deeply flawed. alongside him, andrew garfield as eduardo savarin shines as the loyal yet ultimately betrayed co-founder, delivering a performance that’s equal parts heartbreaking and infuriating. armie hammer’s dual role as cameron and tyler winklevoss is a standout, balancing physical comedy with a biting commentary on privilege and entitlement.\n\nthe script is a marvel of modern screenwriting, packed with rapid-fire exchanges that crackle with wit and venom. sorkin’s writing is dense, intelligent, and relentlessly quotable, elevating the film beyond a simple tech drama into a Shakespearean tragedy of ambition and betrayal. the pacing is relentless, with scenes flowing into one another with a precision that mirrors the cutthroat world of silicon valley.\n\nfincher’s direction is, as always, impeccable. the film is visually stunning, with a cold, clinical aesthetic that mirrors the emotional detachment of its characters. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth is sleek and stylish, using muted colors and precise framing to create a sense of detachment that mirrors zuckerberg’s own emotional distance. the editing, handled by kirk baxter and angus wall, is razor-sharp, with every cut feeling deliberate and purposeful.\n\nthe soundtrack, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is a haunting blend of electronic and orchestral elements that perfectly complements the film’s themes of isolation and ambition. it’s a score that lingers in the mind long after the credits roll, adding an extra layer of unease to an already unsettling story.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a film about a tech company—it’s a meditation on power, betrayal, and the cost of ambition. it’s a story that feels eerily prescient, given the rise of social media and its impact on modern society. the film doesn’t glorify zuckerberg or facebook; instead, it presents them as the inevitable product of a cutthroat, hyper-competitive world where loyalty is a liability and success comes at a price.\n\nif there’s any criticism to be leveled, it’s that the film occasionally glosses over some of the more complex legal and ethical issues surrounding facebook’s creation in favor of a more dramatic narrative. but this is a minor quibble in an otherwise flawless film. the social network is a modern classic, a film that’s as intellectually stimulating as it is entertaining. it’s a rare movie that feels both timeless and urgently of its moment, a scathing indictment of the digital age wrapped in a gripping, character-driven drama.\n\nin short, the social network is one of the best films of the 21st century—a must-watch for anyone interested in cinema, technology, or the darker side of ambition.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.2038457691669464}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.003055786481127143}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -0.6635879278182983}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0005440423847176135}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -5.960446742392378e-06}, {"token": ")", "logprob": -1.0537270307540894}, {"token": " is", "logprob":